<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Grand Duchy of Würzburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1805 CE – 1814 CE · capital Würzburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Napoleonic grand duchy formed from the secularised bishopric of Würzburg for Ferdinand of Tuscany, a Habsburg in Napoleon's camp.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-wurzburg">Prince-Bishopric of Würzburg</a> was secularised in 1803 and given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-pressburg-1805">Peace of Pressburg</a> in 1805, Bavaria handed it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-grand-duke-of-tuscany">Ferdinand III</a>, former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duke-of-tuscany">Grand Duke of Tuscany</a>, in exchange for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a>, and it became the Electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a>. Joining the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a> in 1806, it was renamed a grand duchy.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ferdinand, a brother of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i">Francis I</a>, governed from the great baroque <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg-residence">Würzburg Residenz</a>, keeping a cultured court and a largely Catholic administration. The state was obliged to supply troops for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon's</a> campaigns, including the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1812. Wine growing on the Main was central to its economy.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1814 Ferdinand recovered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Tuscany</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> was returned to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria</a>, becoming part of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-franconia">Lower Franconia</a>. Ferdinand's line, the Habsburg-Tuscany branch, ruled in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-florence">Florence</a> until 1859.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/grand-duchy-of-wurzburg.jpg" alt="Grand Duchy of Würzburg" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Grandduchy_of_Wurzburg.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1805 CE – 1814 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Würzburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ferdinand III</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Electorate of Würzburg, Großherzogtum Würzburg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1810&amp;focus=grand-duchy-of-wurzburg" aria-label="Grand Duchy of Würzburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4908 444 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Grand Duchy of Würzburg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5228 700L5304 759M5228 700L5228 700M5228 700L5199 691M5228 700L5269 749"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 759h0M5228 700h0M5199 691h0M5269 749h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5228 700h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5228 700h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Würzburg. <a href="/atlas?year=1810&amp;focus=grand-duchy-of-wurzburg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
